WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.280
Secure containers start before deployment.

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:08.260
Base image selection brings operating system packages, libraries, defaults, user accounts,

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:11.600
and potential vulnerabilities into the application image.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:16.940
A trusted, minimal, well-maintained base image reduces what the runtime container carries.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:22.040
An image full of unused package managers, shells, debugging tools, stale libraries,

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:26.260
or build artifacts increases both exposure and maintenance burden.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:30.000
Image provenance answers where an image came from, how it was built,

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.140
and whether it should be trusted.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:37.160
Teams should prefer known sources, controlled registries, reviewable build pipelines,

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:40.900
immutable image references where practical, and clear ownership.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.000
Tags are convenient labels, but they can move.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:48.060
Immutability concepts, digests, image signing,

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:52.520
and verification help teams reason about exactly what artifact is being deployed.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:56.580
Dependency management and vulnerability scanning are ongoing practices.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:00.640
Scanning can identify known vulnerable packages in images and dependencies,

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:03.020
but it is not a complete security program.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:08.120
Results need triage, rebuild workflows, exception handling, and ownership.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.980
Software bills of materials, at a high level,

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:15.680
help teams understand what components are present so vulnerability response and compliance

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:19.020
reviews are grounded in inventory rather than guesswork.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:24.360
Runtime images should avoid unnecessary secrets, tools, shells, and build-only content.

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:27.380
Multi-stage builds, careful copy steps,

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:32.000
and clean dependency practices can produce smaller and more focused artifacts.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:35.740
Registry access control is also part of supply chain hygiene:

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:39.240
unauthorized pulls can expose sensitive artifacts,

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:43.060
and unauthorized pushes can introduce untrusted images.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:47.920
The image should be treated as production software, not as a disposable packaging detail.
